WEBVTT


00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.830
Have you ever knit a sweater, put all that
time and energy and money into knitting a

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:07.510
sweater.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.950
Put it on and it just didn't fit.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:17.340
Maybe it was too tight or too sloppy or
just something wasn't right about it.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.390
Well, it's happened to all of us.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.050
Let's spend some time today talking about.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:30.000
Why that sweater didn't fit and what you
can do, so that doesn't happen to you

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.600
again.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:31.980
Let's see.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.220
You have a sweater that
fits that you just love.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.470
Maybe you knit it or you bought it and
this thing just fits you like a glove and

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.300
it's your go to sweater.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.370
Now your friend may come over and look
at you wearing that sweater and think to

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.860
herself, Oh, that's just way too tight.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.350
She won't tell you that, but,

00:00:52.875 --> 00:00:57.135
maybe the one she's wearing, you're
looking at her going, Hmm, that's really

00:00:57.135 --> 00:00:59.625
sloppy and not so attractive.

00:01:00.705 --> 00:01:02.864
Fit is very subjective.

00:01:02.894 --> 00:01:05.025
It's really a personal thing.

00:01:05.504 --> 00:01:11.745
So I can tell you to measure yourself and
make a sweater a certain size and you're

00:01:11.745 --> 00:01:13.275
not going to be happy with it.

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:15.015
But here's the trick.

00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:21.525
Find a sweater that you love, whether it's
in your closet or you find it in a store.

00:01:21.525 --> 00:01:27.045
Go out shop and try some things on
and see what it is that you like.

00:01:27.555 --> 00:01:29.445
Check the body, the fit.

00:01:29.445 --> 00:01:31.065
Does it fit around the bust?

00:01:31.245 --> 00:01:33.675
How about the shoulders or the shoulders?

00:01:33.675 --> 00:01:34.035
Right?

00:01:34.035 --> 00:01:36.075
Are the seams fallen off your shoulders?

00:01:36.675 --> 00:01:38.205
How about the sleeve length?

00:01:38.205 --> 00:01:41.265
Measure your sleeve from your
underarm down to your wrist.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.419
How long do you like your sleeves?

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:48.490
This perfect sweater is going to be just
the right length, maybe just the right

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.729
height of the neck, opening
or depth of the neck opening.

00:01:52.389 --> 00:01:57.520
Find that sweater, be comfortable
with it and take some measurements.

00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:03.555
So tip number one is
don't measure your body.

00:02:03.705 --> 00:02:08.895
Measure a sweater that you like,
which leads us to tip number two.

00:02:09.134 --> 00:02:11.745
So how do you duplicate that sweater?

00:02:12.855 --> 00:02:15.375
The key is choosing your pattern.

00:02:16.395 --> 00:02:20.385
Now, no matter what patterns you use,
whether it's something out of a book or

00:02:20.385 --> 00:02:23.775
something you get off the internet,
or maybe something that's generated in

00:02:23.775 --> 00:02:26.535
software or even a Knit it Now pattern.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.640
It's up to you to determine whether
that pattern is going to meet your

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.300
specifications, your dimensions
that you established.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.170
So you need to, let's say we
take a pattern out of a book.

00:02:41.805 --> 00:02:45.035
Take a look at the diagram,
the finished measurements.

00:02:45.135 --> 00:02:49.215
Now let's not go and talk about
body measurements or anything else.

00:02:49.365 --> 00:02:53.985
You want to know how many stitches are
around the widest part of that body,

00:02:54.045 --> 00:02:54.495
right?

00:02:54.555 --> 00:02:55.575
That sweater.

00:02:56.385 --> 00:03:03.195
So if it has a diagram, check to see what
the widest width this and multiple ply

00:03:03.195 --> 00:03:05.385
that by two to get your bust measurement.

00:03:05.955 --> 00:03:08.835
Then compare that dimension
to your measurements.

00:03:10.095 --> 00:03:14.325
So let's say I want my sweater
to be 40 inches around.

00:03:14.625 --> 00:03:21.015
I will check the pattern to make sure that
the bust size that I choose to knit or the

00:03:21.045 --> 00:03:25.214
sweater size that I choose
to knit is 40 inches around.

00:03:25.364 --> 00:03:28.334
Probably 20 in the front
and 20 in the back.

00:03:28.964 --> 00:03:34.725
If that pattern is 42 or 46
or 38 it's not going to fit.

00:03:34.815 --> 00:03:36.404
I want it to be 40.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:43.410
And if you can do the math and change that
pattern for yourself, Hey, good for you.

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:46.200
If not, don't use that pattern.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.929
Find one that is your size.

00:03:49.079 --> 00:03:53.130
Thanks where software comes in handy, and
that's really where Knit it Now patterns

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.950
come in handy, but we'll talk
more about that in a minute.

00:03:56.549 --> 00:03:58.320
Alright, spoiler alert.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.230
Tips number three, four, and
five, all have to do with gauge.

00:04:02.655 --> 00:04:05.445
Now I'm not going to get on
my soapbox here about gauge.

00:04:05.475 --> 00:04:11.115
I'm going to assume that you knit gage
swatches, and if you're not a believer, I

00:04:11.115 --> 00:04:16.125
will remind you that if you're trying
to reproduce a size, if I'm trying to

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:21.675
reproduce that 40 inches, I need to know
how many stitches it's going to take to

00:04:21.675 --> 00:04:23.145
make that 40 inches.

00:04:23.505 --> 00:04:24.435
Accurately.

00:04:24.495 --> 00:04:25.815
No guesswork.

00:04:26.325 --> 00:04:30.825
If I'm trying to match a gauge to a
specific pattern, the designer came up

00:04:30.825 --> 00:04:33.955
with, I don't know, 16
stitches to the inch.

00:04:34.365 --> 00:04:37.995
My gauge must be 16 stitches to the inch.

00:04:38.085 --> 00:04:44.055
If I'm off by just a little bit, think
about multiplying that around 40 inches,

00:04:44.235 --> 00:04:48.735
and I'm going to get either a sweater that
I'm swimming in or one that I ashamed to

00:04:48.735 --> 00:04:51.075
wear that shows all my lumps and bumps.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:59.490
So if you've had sweaters that don't fit
and you are not religious about matching

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:02.280
gauge or establishing your gauge.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.180
That's probably the number one
reason your sweater didn't fit.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.430
I don't know about you,
but I hate to match gauge.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:18.990
I think I've spent way too many hours over
the years trying to match my gauge to a

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.340
designer's gauge.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:26.730
Let's say I knit something on my machine
and I use a specific yarn and I use

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:30.000
knitted at tension eight
and I get a specific gauge.

00:05:30.645 --> 00:05:35.625
You could take that same Cone of yarn
on your machine at tension 8 and your

00:05:35.625 --> 00:05:36.224
tension.

00:05:36.224 --> 00:05:40.875
Your gauge might be different than mine
and you're going to have to adjust and

00:05:40.875 --> 00:05:41.895
then another swatch.

00:05:42.525 --> 00:05:44.175
Let's not even go there.

00:05:44.385 --> 00:05:48.825
What I prefer to do is to knit a swatch
in my stitch pattern with whatever yarn I

00:05:48.825 --> 00:05:51.735
want to knit, feel how it feels.

00:05:52.965 --> 00:05:58.995
Dress it, see how it washes, see how it
hangs, see how it drapes, and then use my

00:05:58.995 --> 00:06:01.395
gauge and create my own pattern.

00:06:01.695 --> 00:06:03.765
Now you can do that using software.

00:06:03.885 --> 00:06:06.765
You can use pencil and paper
and you can use knit it.

00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:11.235
Now patterns with our
dynamic perfect fit patterns.

00:06:11.325 --> 00:06:13.215
We're going to accomplish two things.

00:06:13.455 --> 00:06:16.395
You're going to be able to use your gauge.

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:19.275
Measure your swatch, your gauge.

00:06:19.575 --> 00:06:24.675
And you'll also be able to perfect fit the
pattern to match those dimensions that you

00:06:24.675 --> 00:06:25.635
established.

00:06:25.875 --> 00:06:30.255
So I will get that 40 inch finished
sweater without doing any math.

00:06:30.984 --> 00:06:33.674
All I need to do is know my gauge.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:40.170
The last tip, or the last step on my
soapbox is to measure, gauge accurately.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:45.570
So many knitters struggle and their
sweaters don't fit because they made a

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.850
mistake in measuring.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:55.080
So this is a skill that you must, must
learn, and here's a little exercise for

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:55.350
you.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.930
You knit your swatch, you measure, you
dress your swatch, which means you wash

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:05.580
it, you steam it, whatever you're going to
do to the final garments, you dress that

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.800
swatch, and then you
take your measurements.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.159
Then I want you to sit down with that same
yarn, same tension settings, everything

00:07:14.159 --> 00:07:20.970
the same at that gauge, and I want you to
knit a rectangle that's, I don't know, six

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.100
by six inches by six inches.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.409
Just choose a size.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:31.140
If your rectangle comes out six
inches by six inches, you're golden.

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.490
You've measured your gauge.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:36.420
Well, you've calculated the
gauge, well, you're all set.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:42.330
If that is not six by six inches,
got to go back to the drawing board.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:43.530
Something's wrong.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:44.130
Now.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.390
Isn't that a whole lot easier than
knitting the whole back of a sweater and

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:50.610
then realizing that maybe
your gauge is wrong?

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:55.260
So I challenge you to do this,
especially when you're new.

00:07:55.905 --> 00:07:59.775
Until you get a feel for
knitting a proper swatch.

00:07:59.985 --> 00:08:05.655
If you're having trouble with swatches,
please go Knititnow.com/Swatch.

00:08:06.045 --> 00:08:11.805
It's filled with tutorials and tips and
tricks about swatching and establishing

00:08:11.805 --> 00:08:12.405
gauge.

00:08:12.585 --> 00:08:17.025
And I'll warn you, I do get up on my
soapbox on this subject in the classroom.

00:08:17.175 --> 00:08:20.325
So for sweaters at fit, you
need to establish your size.

00:08:21.105 --> 00:08:23.955
You need to choose the right pattern.

00:08:25.065 --> 00:08:25.875
And swatch.

00:08:25.935 --> 00:08:27.525
Swatch and swatch.

00:08:28.545 --> 00:08:31.405
Thanks for watching and happy knitting.